[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue with one fix queued. The reports in it hold two causes,
so that fix will not close all of them. Each cause now has its own ticket.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table marks the second cause as having no ticket of its own because it reads only the export.
Its ticket is linked below.

## The two causes

**`parser`:** [ticket 1](https://github.com/WebDevCaveman/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).
The shared parser reads every date month first, and our tenants write the day first. A date comes
out with day and month swapped. This is what #4471 describes.

**`hint`:** [ticket 2](https://github.com/WebDevCaveman/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).
The `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field and then reads the date the
tenant's way. People type what the hint says, and the date comes out swapped. The symptom matches
`parser`, but the fix is different: the hint has to change.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser does not fix the hint. Keep the tickets apart until both fixes ship.
</Callout>

## How to tell them apart

Look at the tenant and the way in, as `src/lib/kb/causes.ts` does:

1. Form report from a tenant on `booking-v3`: `hint`.
2. Any other report from a day-first tenant: `parser`.

The subject line cannot separate them: both causes use the same subjects, which is how they were
merged ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). The intake tag cannot either. It
is a guess from the browser, and the table shows how often it disagrees with the cause.

Count unique report ids. The export pages overlap, so a report can appear twice.

## Still open

The reports in the note under the table read as a one-day move, not a swap. The export does not
show why, so they stay filed by tenant and listed in the `parser` ticket. Someone should read them
again before that ticket closes.

